data:{"id":"67a9cb4a-7c2f-4bd7-ab8c-e00fff92b28d","response_type":"tool_result","content":"","done":false,"data":{"ambiguous_slugs":{"concept/open-source-operating-system":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581","fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"]},"duration_ms":8,"error":"","found_kbs":{"concept/graphical-user-interface":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"concept/multi-tasking-operating-system":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"concept/open-source-operating-system":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581","fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"concept/unix-like-operating-system":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"entity/apple":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"entity/chrome-os":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"entity/freebsd":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"entity/linux":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"entity/linux-mint":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"entity/mac-os":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"entity/macintosh":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"entity/ubuntu":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"entity/unix":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"summary/95bc02d7-d130-4725-8e15-97455995ba58":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"summary/d4fdffd0-5c7d-4290-adbb-f5e1f7ae80b0":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"summary/ee9f60e3-062c-49cb-bad4-d8ccd3e8083c":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"]},"output":"\u003cwiki_page\u003e\n\u003cmetadata\u003e\n\u003cknowledge_base_id\u003efed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581\u003c/knowledge_base_id\u003e\n\u003clink\u003e[[entity/mac-os|Mac OS]]\u003c/link\u003e\n\u003ctype\u003eentity\u003c/type\u003e\n\u003caliases\u003eMac 操作系统, Apple Macintosh 操作系统\u003c/aliases\u003e\n\u003c/metadata\u003e\n\u003crelationships\u003e\n\u003clinks_to\u003e[[entity/apple]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[entity/macintosh]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[concept/graphical-user-interface]] (summary omitted, already seen)\u003c/links_to\u003e\n\u003clinked_from\u003e[[summary/95bc02d7-d130-4725-8e15-97455995ba58]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[entity/apple]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[entity/macintosh]] (summary omitted, already seen)\u003c/linked_from\u003e\n\u003c/relationships\u003e\n\u003csources\u003e\n\u003csource knowledge_id=\"95bc02d7-d130-4725-8e15-97455995ba58\"/\u003e\n\u003c/sources\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003e\nMac OS is the official operating system for Apple Macintosh computers, featuring a graphical user interface with consistent visual elements across all applications.\n\u003c/summary\u003e\n\u003ccontent\u003e\n# Mac OS\n\nMac OS is the formal designation for the operating system developed by [[entity/apple|Apple]] specifically for its line of personal computers. As the core software platform for these machines, it manages hardware resources and provides essential services for computer programs. The system is intrinsically linked to the [[entity/macintosh|Macintosh]] hardware ecosystem, often referred to simply as Mac, ensuring a cohesive experience between the physical device and the software environment.\n\n## User Interface Design\n\nA defining characteristic of Mac OS is its reliance on a [[concept/graphical-user-interface|Graphical User Interface]] (GUI). Unlike command-line interfaces that require users to type text-based commands, the GUI allows users to interact with electronic devices through graphical icons and visual indicators. This approach significantly lowers the barrier to entry for new users and enhances productivity for experienced users by making interactions more intuitive.\n\nThe interface utilizes several key visual metaphors:\n\n*   **Windows**: Rectangular areas on the screen that display the content of applications or files.\n*   **Icons**: Small pictures that represent files, folders, applications, or other objects.\n*   **Consistency**: One of the most notable features of the Graphical User Interface in Mac OS is the uniformity across the platform. All applications running on Macintosh computers share a similar look and feel. This consistency means that once a user learns how to interact with one application, they can easily transfer those skills to other software within the ecosystem.\n\n## Integration with Macintosh Hardware\n\nThe operating system is designed exclusively for Macintosh computers. This tight integration between Mac OS and the underlying hardware allows for optimized performance and stability. By controlling both the hardware and the software, Apple ensures that the Graphical User Interface elements, such as windows and icons, render smoothly and respond quickly to user inputs.\n\nThe standardization of the user interface across all applications on the Macintosh platform reinforces the brand identity and user experience. Whether a user is editing documents, browsing the web, or creating graphics, the fundamental interaction models remain consistent, reducing the learning curve associated with adopting new software tools.\n\u003c/content\u003e\n\u003c/wiki_page\u003e\n\n\u003cwiki_page\u003e\n\u003cmetadata\u003e\n\u003cknowledge_base_id\u003efed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581\u003c/knowledge_base_id\u003e\n\u003clink\u003e[[concept/unix-like-operating-system|Unix-like operating system]]\u003c/link\u003e\n\u003ctype\u003econcept\u003c/type\u003e\n\u003caliases\u003e类 Unix 操作系统\u003c/aliases\u003e\n\u003c/metadata\u003e\n\u003crelationships\u003e\n\u003clinks_to\u003e[[entity/linux]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[entity/freebsd]] (summary omitted, already seen)\u003c/links_to\u003e\n\u003clinked_from\u003e[[summary/d4fdffd0-5c7d-4290-adbb-f5e1f7ae80b0]] (This document compares popular non-Windows operating systems, highlighting Linux distributions like Ubuntu and Mint, and noting the similarities between FreeBSD and Linux despite kernel differences.), [[concept/open-source-operating-system]] (summary omitted, already seen)\u003c/linked_from\u003e\n\u003c/relationships\u003e\n\u003csources\u003e\n\u003csource knowledge_id=\"d4fdffd0-5c7d-4290-adbb-f5e1f7ae80b0\"/\u003e\n\u003c/sources\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003e\nA Unix-like operating system is a category of systems behaving similarly to Unix, including Linux and FreeBSD, which share similar software ecosystems despite kernel differences.\n\u003c/summary\u003e\n\u003ccontent\u003e\n# Unix-like operating system\n\nA Unix-like operating system is a category of operating systems that behave similarly to Unix, including [[entity/linux|Linux]] and [[entity/freebsd|FreeBSD]].\n\nLinux is a Unix-like operating system. Other open-source operating systems include FreeBSD. FreeBSD uses a different kernel, but it uses most of the same software found in typical Linux distributions. The experience of using FreeBSD on a desktop PC is very similar to that of Linux.\n\u003c/content\u003e\n\u003c/wiki_page\u003e\n\n\u003cwiki_page\u003e\n\u003cmetadata\u003e\n\u003cknowledge_base_id\u003efed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581\u003c/knowledge_base_id\u003e\n\u003clink\u003e[[concept/open-source-operating-system|Open-source operating system]]\u003c/link\u003e\n\u003ctype\u003econcept\u003c/type\u003e\n\u003caliases\u003e开源操作系统\u003c/aliases\u003e\n\u003c/metadata\u003e\n\u003crelationships\u003e\n\u003clinks_to\u003e[[entity/linux]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[concept/unix-like-operating-system]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[entity/ubuntu]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[entity/linux-mint]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[entity/freebsd]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[entity/chrome-os]] (summary omitted, already seen)\u003c/links_to\u003e\n\u003clinked_from\u003e[[summary/d4fdffd0-5c7d-4290-adbb-f5e1f7ae80b0]] (summary omitted, already seen)\u003c/linked_from\u003e\n\u003c/relationships\u003e\n\u003csources\u003e\n\u003csource knowledge_id=\"d4fdffd0-5c7d-4290-adbb-f5e1f7ae80b0\"/\u003e\n\u003c/sources\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003e\nThis page defines open-source operating systems as platforms with publicly accessible source code, highlighting Linux distributions like Ubuntu and Mint, as well as FreeBSD.\n\u003c/summary\u003e\n\u003ccontent\u003e\n# Open-source operating system\n\nOpen-source operating systems are software platforms whose source code is publicly accessible.\n\n*   **[[entity/linux|Linux]]**: A [[concept/unix-like-operating-system|Unix-like operating system]] often recommended for users seeking a non-Windows OS for daily PC use. [[entity/ubuntu|Ubuntu]] and [[entity/linux-mint|Linux Mint]] are the most popular distributions within this ecosystem.\n*   **[[entity/freebsd|FreeBSD]]**: An open-source operating system that uses a different kernel compared to Linux. However, it shares much of the same software ecosystem, resulting in a desktop experience very similar to typical Linux distributions.\n*   **[[entity/chrome-os|Chrome OS]]**: Also mentioned as an alternative operating system in the broader landscape of non-Windows platforms.\n\u003c/content\u003e\n\u003c/wiki_page\u003e\n\n\u003cwiki_page\u003e\n\u003cmetadata\u003e\n\u003cknowledge_base_id\u003efed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581\u003c/knowledge_base_id\u003e\n\u003clink\u003e[[concept/multi-tasking-operating-system|Multi-tasking Operating System]]\u003c/link\u003e\n\u003ctype\u003econcept\u003c/type\u003e\n\u003caliases\u003e\u003c/aliases\u003e\n\u003c/metadata\u003e\n\u003crelationships\u003e\n\u003clinks_to\u003e[[entity/unix]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[entity/linux]] (summary omitted, already seen)\u003c/links_to\u003e\n\u003clinked_from\u003e[[summary/ee9f60e3-062c-49cb-bad4-d8ccd3e8083c]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[entity/unix]] (summary omitted, already seen)\u003c/linked_from\u003e\n\u003c/relationships\u003e\n\u003csources\u003e\n\u003csource knowledge_id=\"ee9f60e3-062c-49cb-bad4-d8ccd3e8083c\"/\u003e\n\u003c/sources\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003e\nA multi-tasking operating system enables the concurrent execution of multiple processes or tasks, a capability found in systems like UNIX and Linux.\n\u003c/summary\u003e\n\u003ccontent\u003e\n# Multi-tasking Operating System\n\n**Multi-tasking Operating System**: An operating system capability allowing multiple tasks or processes to be executed concurrently on a computer.\n\n[[entity/unix|UNIX]] is a multi-tasking command-line operating system implemented on many different types of computers. Because it functions as both an independent operating system and a network operating system, UNIX is sometimes referred to as a multi-purpose operating system. [[entity/linux|Linux]] is a popular, free, multi-tasking UNIX-type operating system.\n\u003c/content\u003e\n\u003c/wiki_page\u003e","success":true,"tool_call_id":"call_93072487087943b582059860","tool_name":"wiki_read_page"}}

